WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.100
- And there seems to be no sign

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:03.720
of intelligent life anywhere.

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:04.553
- Hello!

00:00:04.553 --> 00:00:07.563
(light upbeat music)

00:00:15.796 --> 00:00:16.629
- [Narrator] This November,

00:00:16.629 --> 00:00:19.870
"Toy Story" celebrates
its 25-year anniversary.

00:00:19.870 --> 00:00:22.850
The beloved animated adventure
of toy box allegiances

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:25.810
was the first feature length
film released by Pixar.

00:00:25.810 --> 00:00:28.110
Since 1995, the studio has gone on

00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:30.160
to win 16 Academy Awards,

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:31.400
11 Grammy Awards,

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:33.590
and 10 Golden Globe Awards.

00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:34.910
But did you know that the first

00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:37.340
computer animated movie
studio was once owned

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:39.150
by Star Wars' George Lucas

00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:41.020
and Apple's Steve Jobs?

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:41.978
Or that their first initial breaks

00:00:41.978 --> 00:00:45.390
included Star Trek and Listerine?

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:46.280
You will!

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:47.820
Join us as we take a look back

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:49.200
at the history of Pixar,

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:51.250
to infinity and beyond!

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:54.040
Pixar started 16 years prior to releasing

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:55.390
its first feature length film

00:00:55.390 --> 00:00:57.760
when George Lucas recruited Ed Catmull

00:00:57.760 --> 00:00:59.731
to lead Lucasfilm's Computer Division,

00:00:59.731 --> 00:01:01.990
Lucas had already worked with Catmull

00:01:01.990 --> 00:01:03.230
whose company Triple-I

00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:06.180
had made the X-wing
fighter for "Star Wars."

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:08.260
Simply called the Graphics
Group at that time,

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:10.120
what Lucas wanted from this arrangement

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:12.120
was advancements in digital film editing

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:14.410
and digital sound editing.

00:01:14.410 --> 00:01:16.640
By 1982, Graphics Group completed

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:19.118
the first completely
computer animated sequence

00:01:19.118 --> 00:01:21.337
ever made for a feature film in

00:01:21.337 --> 00:01:23.800
"Star Trek II: The Wrath of Khan."

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:25.990
The scene in question was
a terraforming sequence

00:01:25.990 --> 00:01:27.380
by the Genesis Device,

00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:29.240
where we witness a lifeless planet

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:31.760
suddenly transform in simulation.

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:33.760
In 1984, the studio created

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:35.617
its first short film called

00:01:35.617 --> 00:01:38.280
"The Adventures of Andre and Wally B."

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:40.080
Combining hand-painted textures

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:42.520
with flexible
computer-generated characters,

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:44.020
this would be the first example

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:46.750
of character animation done by computers.

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:48.490
The Graphics Group completed one more

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:50.510
feature film sequence for George Lucas,

00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:51.470
the stained-glass knight

00:01:51.470 --> 00:01:52.970
in "Young Sherlock Holmes,"

00:01:52.970 --> 00:01:55.890
before Steve Jobs purchased
the company in 1986,

00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:58.700
one year after being
ousted from MacIntosh.

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:01.410
Jobs bought the Graphics
Group for $5 million

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:03.500
and was the largest
shareholder in the company

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:06.700
until Disney purchased Pixar in 2006.

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:09.110
It was under Jobs that
Pixar first got its name,

00:02:09.110 --> 00:02:10.420
a combination of "pixer,"

00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:11.590
a made up Spanish word

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:13.420
that sounded like "to make pictures,"

00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:15.780
and "radar" for its futuristic learnings.

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:17.140
But it also became the company

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:18.060
that we think of today,

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:20.300
because this was also when the Pixar lamp

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:23.950
was first introduced in
the short film, "Luxo Jr."

00:02:23.950 --> 00:02:25.010
For the first five years

00:02:25.010 --> 00:02:26.930
as an independent animation company,

00:02:26.930 --> 00:02:28.820
Pixar completed a number of shorts

00:02:28.820 --> 00:02:30.565
including 1988's "Tin Toy"

00:02:30.565 --> 00:02:32.880
and 1989's "Knick Knack,"

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:34.690
which saw them making large leaps

00:02:34.690 --> 00:02:36.550
in three-dimensional movement.

00:02:36.550 --> 00:02:39.520
By 1991, Pixar also made commercials

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:41.570
for Trident and Listerine.

00:02:41.570 --> 00:02:42.650
It was the commercial work

00:02:42.650 --> 00:02:44.690
that allowed them to hire
future film directors

00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:47.270
Andrew Stanton and Pete Docter.

00:02:47.270 --> 00:02:49.440
It was also the commercial work pipeline

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:50.690
that showed the studio could work

00:02:50.690 --> 00:02:52.050
with outside clients

00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:54.430
and this led to Pixar's
first agreement with Disney,

00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:56.057
signed in 1991,

00:02:56.057 --> 00:02:57.847
"to make and distribute at least

00:02:57.847 --> 00:03:00.710
"one computer-generated animated movie."

00:03:00.710 --> 00:03:02.270
That first film, of course,

00:03:02.270 --> 00:03:04.207
would become "Toy Story."

00:03:04.207 --> 00:03:08.100
"Toy Story" was the perfect
story for Pixar in 1995

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:09.210
because their technology

00:03:09.210 --> 00:03:11.900
made all organic objects look plastic,

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:13.810
which was ideal for a story concerning

00:03:13.810 --> 00:03:15.370
the active life of toys

00:03:15.370 --> 00:03:17.400
when humans are not around.

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:19.470
The human rendering was so far behind

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:20.820
that originally the filmmakers

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:23.150
hoped to avoid showing humans altogether,

00:03:23.150 --> 00:03:24.700
outside of limbs.

00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:27.050
but early on faces for the Davis family

00:03:27.050 --> 00:03:28.520
and Sid, the evil neighbor,

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:30.910
were deemed necessary for the audience.

00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:32.030
Though the human characters

00:03:32.030 --> 00:03:33.690
look off to us now,

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:36.070
this only highlights the
monumental achievements

00:03:36.070 --> 00:03:37.810
that Pixar has done in advancing

00:03:37.810 --> 00:03:40.150
its own technology through the years.

00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:42.330
Stanton shaped the story as a buddy movie

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:43.250
and the writing team,

00:03:43.250 --> 00:03:46.066
including Joss Whedon, Alec
Sokolow, and Joel Co-hen,

00:03:46.066 --> 00:03:48.490
added sarcasm that was atypical

00:03:48.490 --> 00:03:50.700
for animated movies at the time.

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:53.023
- I'm just a couple of blocks away.

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:55.800
- [Slinky] If Woody says
it's all right then,

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:57.660
well darn it, it's good enough for me.

00:03:57.660 --> 00:04:00.240
- I don't believe that man's
ever been to medical school.

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:01.663
- Do we have to hold hands?

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:04.750
- You guys think this is a big joke!

00:04:04.750 --> 00:04:07.040
- [Narrator] Pixar decided
to avoid musical numbers

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:09.500
to differentiate itself
from Disney proper.

00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:11.350
However, production would be shut down

00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:12.890
to retool Woody's character

00:04:12.890 --> 00:04:14.490
to make him more likable.

00:04:14.490 --> 00:04:15.500
- Wait a minute.

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:18.150
You don't think I meant to
knock Buzz out the window,

00:04:18.150 --> 00:04:19.480
do you Potato Head?

00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:20.940
- That's Mr. Potato Head to you,

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:22.660
you backstabbing murderer.

00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:23.780
- [Narrator] Stanton tasked the writers

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:25.940
to make Woody and Buzz engaging enough

00:04:25.940 --> 00:04:26.773
that if an audience

00:04:26.773 --> 00:04:29.367
"were stuck in an elevator
with them for 70 minutes

00:04:29.367 --> 00:04:31.420
"it would still be entertaining."

00:04:31.420 --> 00:04:33.910
The resulting film, released in 1995,

00:04:33.910 --> 00:04:36.290
was the highest-grossing film of 1995

00:04:36.290 --> 00:04:38.610
at $192 million domestic

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:41.610
and $361 million worldwide,

00:04:41.610 --> 00:04:43.491
far eclipsing Jobs' expectation

00:04:43.491 --> 00:04:45.100
that it would break even

00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:47.333
if it grossed $75 million.

00:04:47.333 --> 00:04:49.090
Pixar's next film, "A Bug's Life,"

00:04:49.090 --> 00:04:50.420
allowed the company to further

00:04:50.420 --> 00:04:51.770
its 3D character mapping

00:04:51.770 --> 00:04:53.470
via a world without humans,

00:04:53.470 --> 00:04:55.170
and "Monsters, Inc." allowed them

00:04:55.170 --> 00:04:57.010
to animate many different shapes

00:04:57.010 --> 00:04:59.340
and improve upon their human forms.

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:00.500
The directive at Pixar

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:03.000
was to create a
computer-animated environment

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:04.640
that had not done before

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:06.630
and thus, the team went underwater

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:09.650
with "Finding Nemo" in 2003.

00:05:09.650 --> 00:05:12.210
- [Group] Fish are friends, not food.

00:05:12.210 --> 00:05:13.663
- Except stinking dolphins.

00:05:13.663 --> 00:05:14.600
- Dolphins?

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:16.800
Yeah, they think they're so cute.

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:17.633
"Oh, look at me.

00:05:17.633 --> 00:05:18.697
"I'm a flipping little dolphin.

00:05:18.697 --> 00:05:19.530
"Let me flip for you.

00:05:19.530 --> 00:05:20.660
"Ain't I something?"

00:05:20.660 --> 00:05:23.360
- [Narrator] In 2004, Pixar's
next landmark achievement

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:25.110
was the three-dimensional human form

00:05:25.110 --> 00:05:26.420
in "The Incredibles,"

00:05:26.420 --> 00:05:27.570
which then blew the door

00:05:27.570 --> 00:05:30.942
off the type of stories Pixar could tell.

00:05:30.942 --> 00:05:32.074
- Honey!

00:05:32.074 --> 00:05:32.922
- [Honey] What?

00:05:32.922 --> 00:05:34.570
- Where's my super suit?

00:05:34.570 --> 00:05:35.403
- [Honey] What?

00:05:35.403 --> 00:05:37.418
- Where is my super suit!

00:05:38.503 --> 00:05:40.902
- [Narrator] I put it away.

00:05:40.902 --> 00:05:41.923
- Where!

00:05:41.923 --> 00:05:43.854
- [Honey] Why do you need to know?

00:05:43.854 --> 00:05:44.754
- I need it!

00:05:44.754 --> 00:05:47.034
- [Narrator] Following that
film's massive success,

00:05:47.034 --> 00:05:49.440
in 2006 the animation studio

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:53.273
was formally sold to
Disney for $7.4 billion.

00:05:54.150 --> 00:05:55.230
Since "The Incredibles,"

00:05:55.230 --> 00:05:57.090
Pixar has created the digital worlds

00:05:57.090 --> 00:05:58.910
of Radiator Springs in "Cars,"

00:05:58.910 --> 00:06:00.870
a French restaurant in "Ratatouille,"

00:06:00.870 --> 00:06:02.650
outer space in "WALL-E,"

00:06:02.650 --> 00:06:05.030
into the sky in "Up's" balloon house,

00:06:05.030 --> 00:06:07.600
to Scotland in "Brave,"

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:10.300
to the Jurassic era in
"The Good Dinosaur,"

00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:13.033
inside a child's brain in "Inside Out,"

00:06:13.033 --> 00:06:15.540
into the afterlife in "Coco,"

00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:18.760
and a world of mythical
creatures in "Onward."

00:06:18.760 --> 00:06:20.060
Over the past 25 years,

00:06:20.060 --> 00:06:21.330
there have been eight sequels

00:06:21.330 --> 00:06:23.209
to five original franchises.

00:06:23.209 --> 00:06:25.270
"The Incredibles II" has been the biggest

00:06:25.270 --> 00:06:26.630
success at the box office

00:06:26.630 --> 00:06:29.459
with $1.2 billion earned worldwide

00:06:29.459 --> 00:06:31.070
and "WALL-E" holds the record

00:06:31.070 --> 00:06:32.677
for most Oscar nominations

00:06:32.677 --> 00:06:35.040
for a Pixar movie at six.

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:38.580
All told, Pixar has earned $14.5 billion

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:40.010
at the worldwide box office

00:06:40.010 --> 00:06:42.750
in addition to all those golden statues.

00:06:42.750 --> 00:06:44.210
Because of the COVID pandemic,

00:06:44.210 --> 00:06:45.910
their next original film, "Soul,"

00:06:45.910 --> 00:06:47.960
will be released on Disney Plus

00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:49.350
but there are four additional

00:06:49.350 --> 00:06:51.360
theatrical Pixar movies in the works

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:55.990
with releases in 2021, 2022, and 2023.

00:06:55.990 --> 00:06:58.117
However, only Enrico Casarosa's

00:06:58.117 --> 00:06:59.990
"Luca" has a title and plot

00:06:59.990 --> 00:07:02.180
as of this video's publication date.

00:07:02.180 --> 00:07:04.010
Set in a beautiful seaside town

00:07:04.010 --> 00:07:05.230
on the Italian Riveria

00:07:05.230 --> 00:07:06.830
where all is idyllic,

00:07:06.830 --> 00:07:09.807
except for the sea monster
that lives offshore.

00:07:09.807 --> 00:07:13.530
"Luca" will be Pixar's
24th feature length film.

00:07:13.530 --> 00:07:15.100
So, of all of Pixar's releases,

00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:16.590
which is your favorite?

00:07:16.590 --> 00:07:18.263
Tell us in the comments below.

